```

As the comment says, my change checks if the var's init expression can be folded to an r-value. Unfortunately, this doesn't handle HLSL-specific cases (yet?). In particular, this change makes the `staticGlobals3.hlsl` test (`tools\clang\test\HLSLFileCheck\hlsl\types\modifiers\static\staticGlobals3.hlsl`):

```hlsl
static float4 f0 = {5,6,7,8};
static float4 f1 = 3;
static float4 f2 = {0,0,0,0};
static float4 f3[] = { f0, f1, f2 };

static float2x2 m2[4] = { 25,26,27,28,
                       25,26,27,28,
                       25,26,27,28,
                       25,26,27,28 };

struct T {
   float2x2 a;
};

struct T2 : T {
   float2 b;
   int2   c;
};

struct T3 : T2 {
   T t[2];
};

static T3 t3[] = { { f0, f2, m2 }, { f1, f3, f2, f0} };
...
```

When evaluating the initializer for t3, my change to check if the init expression `isEvaluatable` fails when it evaluates `m2` (in `{ { f0, f2, m2 }, { f1, f3, f2, f0} }`). I think it should succeed, but is failing because more HLSL-specific changes need to be made to allow constant-folding of these const init lists. However, at this point, I feel well beyond my depth, and will need to stop here.

I hope this exploration helps to figure out the proper fix.
